# Credentials shared by every migrated app — LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY.
#
# Imported by each config/1password/<app>/.env.schema, so the token is declared
# once here instead of six times. See config/1password/README.md for the model.
#
# NOTE: decorator names must never appear with a leading at-sign in prose here.
# Varlock parses every at-token in a comment block as a decorator, so a sentence
# mentioning one is read as a redeclaration with the sentence as its value.
#
# Root decorators are per-file and do NOT propagate through an import, so
# defaultRequired is set here for this file's own items and repeated in each
# importing file for theirs.

# @defaultRequired=infer
# ---

# Scoped, read-only 1Password service account token, read out of the macOS login
# Keychain by the system `security` tool.
#
# Without it, resolution falls back to desktop-app authentication, which
# authorises PER RUN -- a prompt on every `nx serve`. Desktop auth also connects
# as the developer, with their full vault access, where the service account is
# limited to the environments it actually needs.
#
# Create the item once, then approve the first read with ALWAYS ALLOW:
#
#   varlock keychain set OP_TOKEN --account dmr.is:local:OP_TOKEN
#   security find-generic-password -s varlock -a dmr.is:local:OP_TOKEN -w
#
# The second command raises the Keychain ACL dialog. Click **Always Allow**, not
# Allow: that adds /usr/bin/security to the item's trusted-application list, and
# it is the only reason this is a one-time step. Nothing else is needed -- no
# fix-access, no partition list, no per-app setup.
#
# WHY exec() RATHER THAN keychain() OR varlock(), both of which were tried and
# rejected: varlock's own resolvers route every read through its native helper,
# and on macOS that helper is gated by Secure Enclave user presence. Its dialog
# offers no "Always Allow", so it CANNOT be granted persistently -- keychain()
# prompted on every run and varlock() once per terminal, regardless of the item's
# ACL, its partition list, or `varlock keychain fix-access`. The varlock docs are
# explicit that there is no unattended decrypt on macOS, because Secure Enclave
# keys never leave the enclave. Reading the item with `security` bypasses the
# helper entirely, so the ordinary ACL grant applies -- and holds.
#
# The command is guarded so it yields an empty value instead of failing where
# `security` does not exist. This is the one macOS-only piece left, and the guard
# is what keeps it from being a hard block: a developer on Linux or Windows
# resolves nothing here and works through desktop app auth instead.
#
# The service account needs read access to EVERY environment it resolves: the
# shared one plus each app's. Missing that, resolution fails at the first
# setValuesBulk line with a generic "an unexpected error occurred while
# processing the request", which looks nothing like a permissions problem and
# cost an afternoon.
# @type=opServiceAccountToken @internal
OP_TOKEN=exec(`security find-generic-password -s varlock -a dmr.is:local:OP_TOKEN -w 2>/dev/null || true`)
